                "content": "# Solution\n\n## Part (1): Expressions for $K_{\\rm eq}$, $k_P$, $k_Q$, and $k_{\\rm obs}$\n\n**Equilibrium constant.** From $\\Delta G^\\circ = \\Delta H^\\circ - T\\Delta S^\\circ$ and $K_{\\rm eq} = e^{-\\Delta G^\\circ/RT}$:\n\n$$\\boxed{K_{\\rm eq}(T) = \\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{\\Delta S^\\circ_{\\rm eq}}{R}\\right)\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ_{\\rm eq}}{RT}\\right)}$$\n\n**Elementary rate constants** (Eyring equation):\n\n$$k_P(T) = \\frac{k_{\\rm B}T}{h}\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{\\Delta S_P^\\ddagger}{R}\\right)\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger}{RT}\\right)$$\n\n$$k_Q(T) = \\frac{k_{\\rm B}T}{h}\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{\\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger}{R}\\right)\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger}{RT}\\right)$$\n\n**Pseudo-first-order disappearance constant.** With $[I] = K_{\\rm eq}[A]$ and $[A]_{\\rm T} = [A](1+K_{\\rm eq})$:\n\n$$[A] = \\frac{[A]_{\\rm T}}{1+K_{\\rm eq}}, \\qquad [I] = \\frac{K_{\\rm eq}[A]_{\\rm T}}{1+K_{\\rm eq}}$$\n\nThe net rate of loss of total reactant is the sum of the two irreversible steps:\n\n$$-\\frac{d[A]_{\\rm T}}{dt} = k_Q[A] + k_P[I] = \\frac{k_Q + K_{\\rm eq}\\,k_P}{1+K_{\\rm eq}}\\,[A]_{\\rm T}$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\\boxed{k_{\\rm obs}(T) = \\frac{k_Q(T) + K_{\\rm eq}(T)\\,k_P(T)}{1 + K_{\\rm eq}(T)}}$$\n\n---\n\n## Part (2): Product-rate ratio and crossover temperature\n\nThe instantaneous rates of product formation are:\n\n$$r_P = k_P[I] = \\frac{K_{\\rm eq}\\,k_P}{1+K_{\\rm eq}}[A]_{\\rm T}, \\qquad r_Q = k_Q[A] = \\frac{k_Q}{1+K_{\\rm eq}}[A]_{\\rm T}$$\n\nThe ratio simplifies (the common prefactor cancels):\n\n$$\\boxed{\\frac{r_P}{r_Q} = \\frac{K_{\\rm eq}\\,k_P}{k_Q} = \\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{\\Delta S_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta S^\\circ_{\\rm eq} - \\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger}{R}\\right)\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{\\rm eq} - \\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger}{RT}\\right)}$$\n\nSubstituting the numerical values:\n\n$$\\Delta S^* \\equiv \\Delta S_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta S^\\circ_{\\rm eq} - \\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger = -20 + 40 - 30 = -10 \\;\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}$$\n\n$$\\Delta H^* \\equiv \\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{\\rm eq} - \\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger = 45.0 + 20.0 - 70.0 = -5.00 \\;\\mathrm{kJ\\,mol^{-1}}$$\n\nSetting $r_P = r_Q$ requires $\\Delta S^*/R + (-\\Delta H^*)/(RT) = 0$, giving:\n\n$$T = \\frac{\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{\\rm eq} - \\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger}{\\Delta S_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta S^\\circ_{\\rm eq} - \\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger} = \\frac{-5000\\;\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}}{-10\\;\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}}}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{T_{r_P=r_Q} = 500\\;\\mathrm{K}}$$\n\n---\n\n## Part (3): Numerical values at $T = 500.0$ K\n\n**Prefactor:**\n\n$$\\frac{k_{\\rm B}T}{h} = \\frac{1.380649\\times10^{-23}\\times 500.0}{6.62607015\\times10^{-34}} = 1.0418\\times10^{13}\\;\\mathrm{s^{-1}}$$\n\n**Equilibrium constant:**\n\n$$\\frac{\\Delta S^\\circ_{\\rm eq}}{R} = \\frac{40.00}{8.3145} = 4.8109, \\qquad \\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ_{\\rm eq}}{RT} = \\frac{20000}{8.3145\\times 500.0} = 4.8109$$\n\n$$\\boxed{K_{\\rm eq}(500\\;\\mathrm{K}) = e^{4.8109 - 4.8109} = 1.000}$$\n\n**Rate constants.** For $k_P$:\n\n$$\\frac{\\Delta S_P^\\ddagger}{R} = \\frac{-20.00}{8.3145} = -2.4054, \\qquad \\frac{\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger}{RT} = \\frac{45000}{4157.2} = 10.825$$\n\n$$k_P = 1.0418\\times10^{13}\\times e^{-2.4054}\\times e^{-10.825} = 1.0418\\times10^{13}\\times e^{-13.230}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{k_P(500\\;\\mathrm{K}) = 1.87\\times10^{7}\\;\\mathrm{s^{-1}}}$$\n\nFor $k_Q$:\n\n$$\\frac{\\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger}{R} = \\frac{30.00}{8.3145} = 3.6082, \\qquad \\frac{\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger}{RT} = \\frac{70000}{4157.2} = 16.838$$\n\n$$k_Q = 1.0418\\times10^{13}\\times e^{3.6082}\\times e^{-16.838} = 1.0418\\times10^{13}\\times e^{-13.230}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{k_Q(500\\;\\mathrm{K}) = 1.87\\times10^{7}\\;\\mathrm{s^{-1}}}$$\n\n(The equality $k_P = k_Q$ at 500 K is expected: it follows from $K_{\\rm eq}=1$ and $r_P/r_Q = 1$.)\n\n**Observed rate constant:**\n\n$$k_{\\rm obs} = \\frac{k_Q + K_{\\rm eq}\\,k_P}{1+K_{\\rm eq}} = \\frac{1.87\\times10^{7} + 1.87\\times10^{7}}{2}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{k_{\\rm obs}(500\\;\\mathrm{K}) = 1.87\\times10^{7}\\;\\mathrm{s^{-1}}}$$\n\n**Branching fractions:**\n\n$$f_P = \\frac{K_{\\rm eq}\\,k_P}{k_Q + K_{\\rm eq}\\,k_P} = \\frac{1}{2} = 0.500, \\qquad f_Q = \\frac{k_Q}{k_Q + K_{\\rm eq}\\,k_P} = \\frac{1}{2} = 0.500$$\n\n---\n\n## Part (4): Apparent activation energy\n\nStarting from $k_{\\rm obs} = \\dfrac{k_B T}{h}\\cdot\\dfrac{N(T)}{D(T)}$ where\n\n$$N = B_Q\\,e^{-\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger/RT} + B_P\\,e^{-(\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger+\\Delta H^\\circ_{\\rm eq})/RT}, \\qquad D = 1 + B_{\\rm eq}\\,e^{-\\Delta H^\\circ_{\\rm eq}/RT}$$\n\nwith $B_Q = e^{\\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger/R}$, $B_P = e^{(\\Delta S^\\circ_{\\rm eq}+\\Delta S_P^\\ddagger)/R}$, $B_{\\rm eq} = e^{\\Delta S^\\circ_{\\rm eq}/R}$.\n\nTaking $\\beta = 1/T$ and noting $\\frac{d\\ln T}{d\\beta} = -T$:\n\n$$\\frac{d\\ln k_{\\rm obs}}{d\\beta} = -T - \\frac{1}{R}\\cdot\\frac{\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger\\, B_Q e^{-\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger\\beta/R} + (\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger+\\Delta H^\\circ_{\\rm eq})\\,B_P e^{-(\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger+\\Delta H^\\circ_{\\rm eq})\\beta/R}}{B_Q e^{-\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger\\beta/R} + B_P e^{-(\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger+\\Delta H^\\circ_{\\rm eq})\\beta/R}} + \\frac{1}{R}\\cdot\\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ_{\\rm eq}\\,B_{\\rm eq}e^{-\\Delta H^\\circ_{\\rm eq}\\beta/R}}{1+B_{\\rm eq}e^{-\\Delta H^\\circ_{\\rm eq}\\beta/R}}$$\n\nMultiplying by $-R$ and recognizing the ratios of the exponential terms as ratios of rate constants:\n\n$$\\boxed{E_{\\rm app}(T) = RT + \\frac{\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger\\;k_Q(T) + \\bigl(\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{\\rm eq}\\bigr)\\;K_{\\rm eq}(T)\\;k_P(T)}{k_Q(T) + K_{\\rm eq}(T)\\;k_P(T)} - \\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ_{\\rm eq}\\;K_{\\rm eq}(T)}{1+K_{\\rm eq}(T)}}$$\n\n**Evaluation at $T = 500.0$ K** (where $K_{\\rm eq}=1$, $k_P = k_Q$):\n\n| Term | Value (J mol$^{-1}$) |\n|------|------|\n| $RT$ | $4157.2$ |\n| Weighted barrier: $\\frac{70000\\,k + 65000\\,k}{2k}$ | $67\\,500$ |\n| Pre-equilibrium correction: $\\frac{20000\\times1}{2}$ | $-10\\,000$ |\n\n$$E_{\\rm app}(500\\;\\mathrm{K}) = 4157 + 67500 - 10000 = 61\\,657\\;\\mathrm{J\\,mol^{-1}}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{E_{\\rm app}(500\\;\\mathrm{K}) = 61.66\\;\\mathrm{kJ\\,mol^{-1}}}$$\n\n> **Note on the $RT$ term.** This arises because the Eyring prefactor $k_{\\rm B}T/h$ introduces a $T$-dependence beyond the Arrhenius exponential. For a single Eyring step, this gives $E_a = \\Delta H^\\ddagger + RT$\u2014the standard result in transition-state theory.\n\n---\n\n## Part (5): Isotopically substituted reactant\n\n**Modified parameters:**\n\n$$\\Delta H_{P,\\rm iso}^\\ddagger = 49.00\\;\\mathrm{kJ\\,mol^{-1}}, \\qquad \\Delta H_{Q,\\rm iso}^\\ddagger = 71.00\\;\\mathrm{kJ\\,mol^{-1}}$$\n\nAll entropies and the pre-equilibrium are unchanged.\n\n**New crossover temperature.** The condition $r_P = r_Q$ now requires:\n\n$$T_{\\rm iso} = \\frac{\\Delta H_{P,\\rm iso}^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{\\rm eq} - \\Delta H_{Q,\\rm iso}^\\ddagger}{\\Delta S_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta S^\\circ_{\\rm eq} - \\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger} = \\frac{49000 + 20000 - 71000}{-10} = \\frac{-2000}{-10}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{T_{r_P=r_Q}\\bigg|_{\\rm iso} = 200\\;\\mathrm{K}}$$\n\n**Ratio at 500 K:**\n\n$$\\frac{r_P}{r_Q}\\bigg|_{500\\,\\mathrm{K},\\,\\rm iso} = \\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{-10}{8.3145} + \\frac{2000}{8.3145\\times 500.0}\\right) = \\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{-10+4}{8.3145}\\right) = e^{-0.7216}$$\n\n$$\\boxed{r_P/r_Q\\big|_{\\rm iso,\\;500\\,K} = 0.486}$$\n\n**Physical explanation.** The isotope substitution raises the activation enthalpy of the P pathway by 4.0 kJ/mol but only the Q pathway by 1.0 kJ/mol. The P pathway (which proceeds through the intermediate I) is more sensitive to isotopic substitution\u2014likely because the transition state for $I\\to P$ involves a bond that is directly broken or formed at the isotopic site. Since the pre-equilibrium $A\\rightleftharpoons I$ is unaffected (by assumption), the selectivity change is entirely due to the differential shift in the two competing activation barriers. Before substitution, the P pathway had a lower effective barrier ($\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{\\rm eq} = 65$ kJ/mol) than the Q pathway ($70$ kJ/mol). After substitution, the effective barriers become $69$ kJ/mol (P) and $71$ kJ/mol (Q)\u2014still favoring P, but by a smaller margin. At 500 K the ratio drops from 1.0 to 0.486, meaning the isotope effect shifts selectivity substantially toward Q, even though the concentration of the reactive intermediate I relative to A is unchanged.",
                "reasoning_content": "This is a complex physical chemistry problem involving reaction kinetics with a pre-equilibrium. Let me work through each part systematically.\n\n**Part (1): Derive expressions for $K_{eq}(T)$, $k_P(T)$, $k_Q(T)$, and $k_{obs}(T)$.**\n\nThe pre-equilibrium is:\n$$A \\rightleftharpoons I$$\n\nThe equilibrium constant is:\n$$K_{eq}(T) = \\frac{[I]}{[A]} = \\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\Delta G^\\circ_{eq}}{RT}\\right) = \\exp\\left(\\frac{\\Delta S^\\circ_{eq}}{R}\\right)\\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}}{RT}\\right)$$\n\nFor the rate constants using the Eyring equation:\n$$k_P(T) = \\frac{k_B T}{h} \\exp\\left(\\frac{\\Delta S_P^\\ddagger}{R}\\right)\\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger}{RT}\\right)$$\n\n$$k_Q(T) = \\frac{k_B T}{h} \\exp\\left(\\frac{\\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger}{R}\\right)\\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger}{RT}\\right)$$\n\nNow for the rate law. We have:\n- $[A]_T = [A] + [I]$\n- $[I] = K_{eq}[A]$\n- So $[A]_T = [A](1 + K_{eq})$, which gives $[A] = \\frac{[A]_T}{1+K_{eq}}$ and $[I] = \\frac{K_{eq}[A]_T}{1+K_{eq}}$\n\nThe rate of disappearance of $[A]_T$:\n$$-\\frac{d[A]_T}{dt} = -\\frac{d([A]+[I])}{dt}$$\n\nSince $[I] = K_{eq}[A]$ (pre-equilibrium is maintained), we have:\n$$-\\frac{d[A]_T}{dt} = (1+K_{eq})\\left(-\\frac{d[A]}{dt}\\right)$$\n\nNow, the rate of loss of A:\n- A is consumed to form Q: rate $= k_Q[A]$\n- A is converted to I (but this is just redistribution within $[A]_T$)\n\nActually, let me think more carefully. The rate of loss of A from the system:\n- $A \\rightarrow Q$ with rate $k_Q[A]$\n- $A \\rightarrow I$ (forward) with rate $k_f[A]$\n- $I \\rightarrow A$ (reverse) with rate $k_r[I]$\n- $I \\rightarrow P$ with rate $k_P[I]$\n\nThe net rate of change of A:\n$$\\frac{d[A]}{dt} = -k_f[A] + k_r[I] - k_Q[A]$$\n\nThe net rate of change of I:\n$$\\frac{d[I]}{dt} = k_f[A] - k_r[I] - k_P[I]$$\n\nSo:\n$$\\frac{d[A]_T}{dt} = \\frac{d[A]}{dt} + \\frac{d[I]}{dt} = -k_Q[A] - k_P[I]$$\n\nTherefore:\n$$-\\frac{d[A]_T}{dt} = k_Q[A] + k_P[I] = k_Q \\frac{[A]_T}{1+K_{eq}} + k_P \\frac{K_{eq}[A]_T}{1+K_{eq}}$$\n\n$$-\\frac{d[A]_T}{dt} = \\frac{k_Q + K_{eq} k_P}{1+K_{eq}} [A]_T$$\n\nSo:\n$$k_{obs}(T) = \\frac{k_Q + K_{eq} \\cdot k_P}{1 + K_{eq}}$$\n\n**Part (2): Instantaneous product-rate ratio $r_P/r_Q$ and temperature where $r_P = r_Q$.**\n\nThe rates of formation:\n$$r_P = k_P[I] = k_P \\frac{K_{eq}[A]_T}{1+K_{eq}}$$\n$$r_Q = k_Q[A] = k_Q \\frac{[A]_T}{1+K_{eq}}$$\n\nSo:\n$$\\frac{r_P}{r_Q} = \\frac{k_P \\cdot K_{eq}}{k_Q}$$\n\nLet me compute this:\n$$\\frac{r_P}{r_Q} = \\frac{k_P \\cdot K_{eq}}{k_Q}$$\n\n$$= \\frac{\\frac{k_B T}{h} e^{\\Delta S_P^\\ddagger/R} e^{-\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger/RT} \\cdot e^{\\Delta S^\\circ_{eq}/R} e^{-\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}/RT}}{\\frac{k_B T}{h} e^{\\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger/R} e^{-\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger/RT}}$$\n\n$$= \\frac{e^{\\Delta S_P^\\ddagger/R} e^{\\Delta S^\\circ_{eq}/R} e^{-\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger/RT} e^{-\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}/RT}}{e^{\\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger/R} e^{-\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger/RT}}$$\n\n$$= \\exp\\left(\\frac{\\Delta S_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta S^\\circ_{eq} - \\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger}{R}\\right) \\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq} - \\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger}{RT}\\right)$$\n\nLet me define:\n$$\\Delta S^* = \\Delta S_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta S^\\circ_{eq} - \\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger = -20 + 40 - 30 = -10 \\text{ J mol}^{-1}\\text{K}^{-1}$$\n$$\\Delta H^* = \\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq} - \\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger = 45 + 20 - 70 = -5 \\text{ kJ mol}^{-1} = -5000 \\text{ J mol}^{-1}$$\n\nSo:\n$$\\frac{r_P}{r_Q} = \\exp\\left(\\frac{-10}{R}\\right) \\exp\\left(\\frac{5000}{RT}\\right)$$\n\nFor $r_P = r_Q$:\n$$\\exp\\left(\\frac{-10}{R}\\right) \\exp\\left(\\frac{5000}{RT}\\right) = 1$$\n\n$$\\frac{-10}{R} + \\frac{5000}{RT} = 0$$\n\n$$\\frac{5000}{RT} = \\frac{10}{R}$$\n\n$$T = \\frac{5000}{10} = 500 \\text{ K}$$\n\nSo the temperature where $r_P = r_Q$ is $T = 500$ K.\n\n**Part (3): Calculate values at $T = 500.0$ K.**\n\n$R = 8.3144626$ J mol$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$\n$k_B = 1.380649 \\times 10^{-23}$ J K$^{-1}$\n$h = 6.62607015 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s\n\nFirst, $k_B T / h$:\n$$\\frac{k_B T}{h} = \\frac{1.380649 \\times 10^{-23} \\times 500.0}{6.62607015 \\times 10^{-34}} = \\frac{6.903245 \\times 10^{-21}}{6.62607015 \\times 10^{-34}} = 1.04184 \\times 10^{13} \\text{ s}^{-1}$$\n\nLet me be more precise:\n$$\\frac{1.380649 \\times 500.0}{6.62607015} \\times 10^{-23+34} = \\frac{690.3245}{6.62607015} \\times 10^{11}$$\n\n$690.3245 / 6.62607015 = 104.183...$\n\nSo $k_B T/h = 1.04183 \\times 10^{13}$ s$^{-1}$\n\nActually let me recalculate: $690.3245 / 6.62607015$\n\n$6.62607015 \\times 104 = 689.111...$\n$690.3245 - 689.111 = 1.2135$\n$1.2135 / 6.62607015 = 0.1831$\n\nSo $k_B T/h = 1.04183 \\times 10^{13}$ s$^{-1}$\n\n$K_{eq}(500) = \\exp\\left(\\frac{40.00}{8.3144626}\\right) \\exp\\left(-\\frac{20000}{8.3144626 \\times 500.0}\\right)$\n\n$\\frac{40.00}{8.3144626} = 4.8109...$\n\nLet me compute: $40/8.3144626 = 4.81089...$\n\n$\\frac{20000}{8.3144626 \\times 500} = \\frac{20000}{4157.2313} = 4.81089...$\n\nInteresting! So:\n$K_{eq}(500) = \\exp(4.81089 - 4.81089) = \\exp(0) = 1$\n\nThat's a nice result. Let me verify:\n$\\Delta S^\\circ_{eq}/R = 40.00/8.3144626 = 4.8109...$\n$\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}/(RT) = 20000/(8.3144626 \\times 500) = 20000/4157.2313 = 4.8109...$\n\nYes, they cancel exactly. So $K_{eq}(500) = 1$.\n\nNow $k_P(500)$:\n$\\Delta S_P^\\ddagger/R = -20.00/8.3144626 = -2.40544...$\n$\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger/(RT) = 45000/(8.3144626 \\times 500) = 45000/4157.2313 = 10.8246...$\n\n$k_P = 1.04183 \\times 10^{13} \\times \\exp(-2.40544) \\times \\exp(-10.8246)$\n$= 1.04183 \\times 10^{13} \\times \\exp(-13.2300...)$\n\n$\\exp(-13.2300) = ?$\n\nLet me compute more carefully.\n$-2.40544 - 10.8246 = -13.2300...$\n\nLet me be more precise:\n$-20/8.3144626 = -2.405445...$\n$45000/4157.2313 = 10.82456...$\n\nSum: $-2.405445 - 10.82456 = -13.23000...$\n\n$\\exp(-13.23) = ?$\n\n$\\exp(-13) = 2.2622 \\times 10^{-6}$\n$\\exp(-0.23) = 0.79453$\n$\\exp(-13.23) = 2.2622 \\times 10^{-6} \\times 0.79453 = 1.7974 \\times 10^{-6}$\n\nSo $k_P = 1.04183 \\times 10^{13} \\times 1.7974 \\times 10^{-6} = 1.8726 \\times 10^{7}$ s$^{-1}$\n\nWait, let me be more precise with the exponents.\n\n$-20.00/8.3144626$: \n$8.3144626 \\times 2 = 16.6289252$\n$20 - 16.6289252 = 3.3710748$\n$3.3710748/8.3144626 = 0.40544...$\nSo $-20/8.3144626 = -2.40544...$\n\n$45000/(8.3144626 \\times 500)$:\n$8.3144626 \\times 500 = 4157.2313$\n$45000/4157.2313 = 10.8246...$\n\nLet me compute $45000/4157.2313$:\n$4157.2313 \\times 10 = 41572.313$\n$45000 - 41572.313 = 3427.687$\n$3427.687/4157.2313 = 0.82456...$\nSo $45000/4157.2313 = 10.82456...$\n\nTotal exponent: $-2.40544 - 10.82456 = -13.23000$\n\n$\\exp(-13.23)$:\n$\\ln(10) = 2.302585$\n$13.23/2.302585 = 5.7458...$\nSo $\\exp(-13.23) = 10^{-5.7458}$\n\n$10^{-5} = 10^{-5}$\n$10^{-0.7458} = ?$\n$\\log_{10}(0.1794) \\approx -0.7461$\n\nSo $\\exp(-13.23) \\approx 1.794 \\times 10^{-6}$\n\n$k_P = 1.04183 \\times 10^{13} \\times 1.794 \\times 10^{-6} = 1.869 \\times 10^{7}$ s$^{-1}$\n\nHmm, let me try to be more precise.\n\nActually, let me redo this calculation more carefully.\n\n$k_B T/h = \\frac{1.380649 \\times 10^{-23} \\times 500}{6.62607015 \\times 10^{-34}}$\n\nNumerator: $1.380649 \\times 500 \\times 10^{-23} = 690.3245 \\times 10^{-23} = 6.903245 \\times 10^{-21}$\n\nDenominator: $6.62607015 \\times 10^{-34}$\n\nRatio: $6.903245/6.62607015 \\times 10^{-21+34} = 1.041837... \\times 10^{13}$\n\nLet me compute $6.903245/6.62607015$:\n$6.62607015 \\times 1.04 = 6.89111...$\n$6.903245 - 6.89111 = 0.01213$\n$0.01213/6.62607 = 0.001831$\nSo ratio = $1.041831$\n\n$k_B T/h = 1.041831 \\times 10^{13}$ s$^{-1}$\n\nFor $k_P$:\nExponent = $\\Delta S_P^\\ddagger/R - \\Delta H_P^\\ddagger/(RT) = -20/8.3144626 - 45000/(8.3144626 \\times 500)$\n$= -2.40545 - 10.82456 = -13.23001$\n\n$\\exp(-13.23001)$:\n\nLet me use a different approach. \n$13.23001 / \\ln(10) = 13.23001/2.302585 = 5.74581$\n\nSo $\\exp(-13.23001) = 10^{-5.74581}$\n\n$10^{-5.74581} = 10^{-6} \\times 10^{0.25419}$\n$10^{0.25419} = ?$\n$10^{0.25} = 1.7783$\n$10^{0.00419} \\approx 1 + 0.00419 \\times 2.3026 = 1.00965$\nSo $10^{0.25419} \\approx 1.7783 \\times 1.00965 = 1.7954$\n\n$\\exp(-13.23001) \\approx 1.7954 \\times 10^{-6}$\n\n$k_P = 1.041831 \\times 10^{13} \\times 1.7954 \\times 10^{-6} = 1.8704 \\times 10^{7}$ s$^{-1}$\n\nFor $k_Q$:\nExponent = $\\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger/R - \\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger/(RT) = 30/8.3144626 - 70000/(8.3144626 \\times 500)$\n$= 3.60817 - 16.83673 = -13.22856$\n\nWait: $30/8.3144626 = 3.60817...$\n$70000/4157.2313 = 16.83673...$\n\nLet me verify: $4157.2313 \\times 16 = 66515.7$\n$70000 - 66515.7 = 3484.3$\n$3484.3/4157.2313 = 0.83813$\nSo $70000/4157.2313 = 16.83813$\n\nHmm, let me redo: $8.3144626 \\times 500 = 4157.2313$\n$70000/4157.2313$:\n$4157.2313 \\times 16 = 66515.70$\n$70000 - 66515.70 = 3484.30$\n$3484.30/4157.2313 = 0.83813$\nSo $70000/4157.2313 = 16.83813$\n\nAnd $30/8.3144626$:\n$8.3144626 \\times 3 = 24.94339$\n$30 - 24.94339 = 5.05661$\n$5.05661/8.3144626 = 0.60817$\nSo $30/8.3144626 = 3.60817$\n\nExponent for $k_Q$: $3.60817 - 16.83813 = -13.22996$\n\n$\\exp(-13.22996)$:\n$13.22996/2.302585 = 5.74579$\n$10^{-5.74579} = 10^{-6} \\times 10^{0.25421}$\n$10^{0.25421} \\approx 1.7955$\n\n$\\exp(-13.22996) \\approx 1.7955 \\times 10^{-6}$\n\n$k_Q = 1.041831 \\times 10^{13} \\times 1.7955 \\times 10^{-6} = 1.8705 \\times 10^{7}$ s$^{-1}$\n\nInteresting! At 500 K, $k_P \\approx k_Q$. This makes sense because $K_{eq} = 1$ and $r_P/r_Q = 1$ at 500 K.\n\nWait, let me double-check. We said $r_P/r_Q = k_P K_{eq}/k_Q$. At 500 K, $K_{eq} = 1$ and $r_P/r_Q = 1$, so indeed $k_P = k_Q$ at 500 K.\n\nLet me verify: the exponent for $k_P$ is $-13.23001$ and for $k_Q$ is $-13.22996$. These are very close but not exactly equal. The small difference is due to rounding in my hand calculations. Let me check if they should be exactly equal.\n\nFor $r_P/r_Q = 1$ at $T = 500$ K, we need $k_P K_{eq} = k_Q$, i.e., $k_P = k_Q$ (since $K_{eq} = 1$).\n\n$k_P/k_Q = \\exp\\left(\\frac{\\Delta S_P^\\ddagger - \\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger}{R}\\right) \\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger - \\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger}{RT}\\right)$\n\n$= \\exp\\left(\\frac{-20-30}{8.3144626}\\right) \\exp\\left(-\\frac{45000-70000}{8.3144626 \\times 500}\\right)$\n\n$= \\exp\\left(\\frac{-50}{8.3144626}\\right) \\exp\\left(\\frac{25000}{4157.2313}\\right)$\n\n$= \\exp(-6.01368) \\exp(6.01368)$\n\n$= \\exp(0) = 1$\n\nLet me verify: $50/8.3144626 = 6.01368...$\n$25000/4157.2313 = 6.01368...$\n\n$8.3144626 \\times 6 = 49.88678$\n$50 - 49.88678 = 0.11322$\n$0.11322/8.3144626 = 0.013617$\nSo $50/8.3144626 = 6.013617$\n\n$4157.2313 \\times 6 = 24943.388$\n$25000 - 24943.388 = 56.612$\n$56.612/4157.2313 = 0.013617$\nSo $25000/4157.2313 = 6.013617$\n\nYes! They're exactly equal. So $k_P = k_Q$ at 500 K exactly.\n\nSo let me recompute more carefully:\n\n$k_P = k_Q = \\frac{k_B T}{h} \\exp\\left(\\frac{\\Delta S_P^\\ddagger}{R}\\right) \\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger}{RT}\\right)$\n\n$= 1.041831 \\times 10^{13} \\times \\exp(-2.40545) \\times \\exp(-10.82456)$\n\n$= 1.041831 \\times 10^{13} \\times \\exp(-13.23001)$\n\nNow I need to compute $\\exp(-13.23001)$ more precisely.\n\nActually, let me use a different approach. Let me compute $\\exp(-13.23001)$ using the fact that $\\exp(-13) = 2.26217 \\times 10^{-6}$ and $\\exp(-0.23) = 0.79453$.\n\n$\\exp(-13.23) = 2.26217 \\times 10^{-6} \\times 0.79453 = 1.79739 \\times 10^{-6}$\n\nFor the extra $-0.00001$: $\\exp(-0.00001) \\approx 1 - 0.00001$, negligible.\n\nSo $\\exp(-13.23001) \\approx 1.79739 \\times 10^{-6}$\n\n$k_P = k_Q = 1.041831 \\times 10^{13} \\times 1.79739 \\times 10^{-6} = 1.8726 \\times 10^{7}$ s$^{-1}$\n\nLet me compute: $1.041831 \\times 1.79739 = ?$\n$1.04 \\times 1.79739 = 1.86929$\n$0.001831 \\times 1.79739 = 0.003291$\nTotal: $1.87258$\n\nSo $k_P = k_Q = 1.8726 \\times 10^{7}$ s$^{-1}$ (approximately)\n\nActually, let me be even more careful. Let me recompute $\\exp(-13.23001)$.\n\n$\\exp(-13) = 1/\\exp(13)$\n$\\exp(13) = \\exp(12) \\times \\exp(1) = 162754.79 \\times 2.718282 = 442413.4...$\n\nActually, $\\exp(13) = 442413.392...$\nSo $\\exp(-13) = 2.26033 \\times 10^{-6}$\n\n$\\exp(-0.23) = 1 - 0.23 + 0.23^2/2 - ... \\approx 0.79453$\n\n$\\exp(-13.23) = 2.26033 \\times 10^{-6} \\times 0.79453 = 1.79589 \\times 10^{-6}$\n\n$k_P = 1.041831 \\times 10^{13} \\times 1.79589 \\times 10^{-6}$\n$= 1.041831 \\times 1.79589 \\times 10^{7}$\n$= 1.8709 \\times 10^{7}$ s$^{-1}$\n\nHmm, I'm getting slightly different values due to precision in intermediate steps. Let me try to be more systematic.\n\nLet me compute $\\exp(13)$ more carefully:\n$\\exp(1) = 2.718281828$\n$\\exp(2) = 7.389056099$\n$\\exp(4) = 54.59815003$\n$\\exp(8) = 2980.957987$\n$\\exp(12) = \\exp(8) \\times \\exp(4) = 2980.957987 \\times 54.59815003 = 162754.7914$\n$\\exp(13) = \\exp(12) \\times \\exp(1) = 162754.7914 \\times 2.718281828 = 442413.3920$\n\n$\\exp(-13) = 1/442413.3920 = 2.26033 \\times 10^{-6}$\n\n$\\exp(0.23)$:\n$0.23^2/2 = 0.02645$\n$0.23^3/6 = 0.002028$\n$0.23^4/24 = 0.000117$\n$\\exp(0.23) \\approx 1 + 0.23 + 0.02645 + 0.002028 + 0.000117 = 1.258595$\n$\\exp(-0.23) = 1/1.258595 = 0.794534$\n\n$\\exp(-13.23) = 2.26033 \\times 10^{-6} \\times 0.794534 = 1.79587 \\times 10^{-6}$\n\n$k_P = 1.041831 \\times 10^{13} \\times 1.79587 \\times 10^{-6}$\n\n$1.041831 \\times 1.79587$:\n$1 \\times 1.79587 = 1.79587$\n$0.04 \\times 1.79587 = 0.071835$\n$0.001 \\times 1.79587 = 0.001796$\n$0.000831 \\times 1.79587 = 0.001492$\nTotal: $1.79587 + 0.071835 + 0.001796 + 0.001492 = 1.870993$\n\nSo $k_P = k_Q = 1.871 \\times 10^{7}$ s$^{-1}$\n\nNow $k_{obs}$:\n$$k_{obs} = \\frac{k_Q + K_{eq} \\cdot k_P}{1 + K_{eq}} = \\frac{k_Q + k_P}{2} = k_P = 1.871 \\times 10^{7} \\text{ s}^{-1}$$\n\n(since $K_{eq} = 1$ and $k_P = k_Q$)\n\nBranching fractions:\nFraction to P: $f_P = \\frac{K_{eq} \\cdot k_P}{k_Q + K_{eq} \\cdot k_P} = \\frac{1 \\cdot k_P}{k_Q + k_P} = \\frac{1}{2} = 0.500$\nFraction to Q: $f_Q = \\frac{k_Q}{k_Q + K_{eq} \\cdot k_P} = \\frac{1}{2} = 0.500$\n\n**Part (4): Apparent activation energy.**\n\n$$k_{obs}(T) = \\frac{k_Q + K_{eq} \\cdot k_P}{1 + K_{eq}}$$\n\nLet me write this as:\n$$k_{obs} = \\frac{k_Q}{1+K_{eq}} + \\frac{K_{eq} \\cdot k_P}{1+K_{eq}}$$\n\nLet me define $\\phi = K_{eq}/(1+K_{eq})$ (fraction of A that is in form I).\nThen $1/(1+K_{eq}) = 1 - \\phi = 1/(1+K_{eq})$.\n\nActually, let me just work with the expression directly.\n\n$$k_{obs} = \\frac{k_Q + K_{eq} \\cdot k_P}{1 + K_{eq}}$$\n\nTaking the logarithm:\n$$\\ln k_{obs} = \\ln(k_Q + K_{eq} \\cdot k_P) - \\ln(1 + K_{eq})$$\n\nNow, $k_P = A_P \\exp(-\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger/RT)$ where $A_P = \\frac{k_B T}{h} \\exp(\\Delta S_P^\\ddagger/R)$\n\nSimilarly $k_Q = A_Q \\exp(-\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger/RT)$ where $A_Q = \\frac{k_B T}{h} \\exp(\\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger/R)$\n\nAnd $K_{eq} = \\exp(\\Delta S^\\circ_{eq}/R) \\exp(-\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}/RT) = K_0 \\exp(-\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}/RT)$ where $K_0 = \\exp(\\Delta S^\\circ_{eq}/R)$.\n\nSo:\n$k_P = A_P e^{-a/T}$ where $a = \\Delta H_P^\\ddagger/R$\n$k_Q = A_Q e^{-b/T}$ where $b = \\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger/R$\n$K_{eq} = K_0 e^{-c/T}$ where $c = \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}/R$\n\nThen:\n$k_{obs} = \\frac{A_Q e^{-b/T} + K_0 A_P e^{-(a+c)/T}}{1 + K_0 e^{-c/T}}$\n\nLet me define:\n- $u = e^{-a/T} = e^{-\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger/RT}$\n- $v = e^{-b/T} = e^{-\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger/RT}$\n- $w = e^{-c/T} = e^{-\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}/RT}$\n\nThen:\n$k_{obs} = \\frac{A_Q v + K_0 A_P u w}{1 + K_0 w}$\n\nNow $E_{app} = -R \\frac{d \\ln k_{obs}}{d(1/T)}$\n\nLet $\\beta = 1/T$. Then:\n$u = e^{-a\\beta}$, $v = e^{-b\\beta}$, $w = e^{-c\\beta}$\n\n$\\frac{du}{d\\beta} = -au$\n$\\frac{dv}{d\\beta} = -bv$\n$\\frac{dw}{d\\beta} = -cw$\n\n$\\ln k_{obs} = \\ln(A_Q v + K_0 A_P u w) - \\ln(1 + K_0 w)$\n\n$\\frac{d \\ln k_{obs}}{d\\beta} = \\frac{-b A_Q v - K_0 A_P (a+c) u w}{A_Q v + K_0 A_P u w} - \\frac{-c K_0 w}{1 + K_0 w}$\n\n$= -\\frac{b A_Q v + (a+c) K_0 A_P u w}{A_Q v + K_0 A_P u w} + \\frac{c K_0 w}{1 + K_0 w}$\n\nTherefore:\n$$E_{app} = -R \\frac{d \\ln k_{obs}}{d\\beta} = R\\left[\\frac{b A_Q v + (a+c) K_0 A_P u w}{A_Q v + K_0 A_P u w} - \\frac{c K_0 w}{1 + K_0 w}\\right]$$\n\nSubstituting back:\n$$E_{app} = R\\left[\\frac{\\frac{\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger}{R} A_Q e^{-\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger/RT} + \\frac{\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}}{R} K_0 A_P e^{-(\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq})/RT}}{A_Q e^{-\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger/RT} + K_0 A_P e^{-(\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq})/RT}} - \\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}}{R} \\cdot \\frac{K_0 e^{-\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}/RT}}{1 + K_0 e^{-\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}/RT}}\\right]$$\n\nSimplifying:\n$$E_{app} = \\frac{\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger A_Q e^{-\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger/RT} + (\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}) K_0 A_P e^{-(\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq})/RT}}{A_Q e^{-\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger/RT} + K_0 A_P e^{-(\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq})/RT}} - \\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq} K_0 e^{-\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}/RT}}{1 + K_0 e^{-\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}/RT}}$$\n\nLet me rewrite this in a more physical form. Let me define:\n- $\\alpha = \\frac{K_{eq} k_P}{k_Q + K_{eq} k_P}$ = fraction of the \"effective\" rate coming from the P pathway\n- $1 - \\alpha = \\frac{k_Q}{k_Q + K_{eq} k_P}$ = fraction from the Q pathway\n\nThen the first term becomes:\n$$\\alpha (\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}) + (1-\\alpha) \\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger$$\n\nAnd the second term is:\n$$\\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq} \\cdot K_{eq}}{1 + K_{eq}}$$\n\nSo:\n$$E_{app} = \\alpha (\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}) + (1-\\alpha) \\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger - \\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq} \\cdot K_{eq}}{1 + K_{eq}}$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha = \\frac{K_{eq} k_P}{k_Q + K_{eq} k_P}$.\n\nLet me verify this at $T = 500$ K where $K_{eq} = 1$ and $k_P = k_Q$:\n$\\alpha = \\frac{1 \\cdot k_P}{k_Q + k_P} = 1/2$\n\n$E_{app} = \\frac{1}{2}(45000 + 20000) + \\frac{1}{2}(70000) - \\frac{20000 \\cdot 1}{2}$\n$= \\frac{1}{2}(65000) + 35000 - 10000$\n$= 32500 + 35000 - 10000$\n$= 62500$ J/mol\n$= 62.5$ kJ/mol\n\nLet me double-check this with the full formula.\n\nAt $T = 500$ K:\n$A_P = \\frac{k_B T}{h} \\exp(\\Delta S_P^\\ddagger/R) = 1.041831 \\times 10^{13} \\times \\exp(-2.40545)$\n$A_Q = \\frac{k_B T}{h} \\exp(\\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger/R) = 1.041831 \\times 10^{13} \\times \\exp(3.60817)$\n$K_0 = \\exp(\\Delta S^\\circ_{eq}/R) = \\exp(4.81089)$\n\n$e^{-\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger/RT} = e^{-10.82456}$\n$e^{-\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger/RT} = e^{-16.83813}$\n$e^{-\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}/RT} = e^{-4.81089}$\n\nFirst term numerator:\n$\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger \\cdot A_Q \\cdot e^{-\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger/RT} = 70000 \\times 1.041831 \\times 10^{13} \\times e^{3.60817} \\times e^{-16.83813}$\n$= 70000 \\times 1.041831 \\times 10^{13} \\times e^{-13.22996}$\n$= 70000 \\times k_Q$\n\nSecond term in numerator:\n$(\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}) \\cdot K_0 \\cdot A_P \\cdot e^{-(\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq})/RT}$\n$= 65000 \\times e^{4.81089} \\times 1.041831 \\times 10^{13} \\times e^{-2.40545} \\times e^{-10.82456 - 4.81089}$\n$= 65000 \\times 1.041831 \\times 10^{13} \\times e^{4.81089 - 2.40545 - 10.82456 - 4.81089}$\n$= 65000 \\times 1.041831 \\times 10^{13} \\times e^{-13.23001}$\n$= 65000 \\times k_P$\n\nDenominator:\n$A_Q e^{-\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger/RT} + K_0 A_P e^{-(\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq})/RT}$\n$= k_Q + K_{eq} \\cdot k_P$\n\nSo first term = $\\frac{70000 k_Q + 65000 k_P}{k_Q + k_P}$ (at $K_{eq} = 1$)\n$= \\frac{70000 \\times k + 65000 \\times k}{2k} = \\frac{135000}{2} = 67500$\n\nSecond term: $\\frac{20000 \\times 1}{1+1} = 10000$\n\n$E_{app} = 67500 - 10000 = 57500$ J/mol = 57.5 kJ/mol\n\nWait, that doesn't match my earlier calculation. Let me recheck.\n\nOh wait, I think I made an error. Let me redo.\n\n$\\alpha = \\frac{K_{eq} k_P}{k_Q + K_{eq} k_P} = \\frac{1 \\cdot k}{k + k} = 1/2$\n\n$E_{app} = \\alpha (\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}) + (1-\\alpha) \\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger - \\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq} \\cdot K_{eq}}{1 + K_{eq}}$\n\n$= \\frac{1}{2}(45000 + 20000) + \\frac{1}{2}(70000) - \\frac{20000}{2}$\n\n$= \\frac{1}{2}(65000) + 35000 - 10000$\n\n$= 32500 + 35000 - 10000$\n\n$= 62500$ J/mol\n\nBut from the direct calculation I got 57500. There's a discrepancy. Let me find the error.\n\nLet me recompute the first term more carefully.\n\nFirst term = $\\frac{\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger k_Q + (\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}) K_{eq} k_P}{k_Q + K_{eq} k_P}$\n\nAt $K_{eq} = 1$, $k_P = k_Q = k$:\n$= \\frac{70000 k + 65000 \\cdot k}{k + k} = \\frac{135000 k}{2k} = 67500$\n\nSecond term = $\\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq} K_{eq}}{1 + K_{eq}} = \\frac{20000 \\cdot 1}{2} = 10000$\n\n$E_{app} = 67500 - 10000 = 57500$ J/mol\n\nNow let me recheck the $\\alpha$ formula:\n$E_{app} = \\alpha (\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}) + (1-\\alpha) \\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger - \\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq} K_{eq}}{1+K_{eq}}$\n\nWith $\\alpha = 1/2$:\n$= \\frac{1}{2}(65000) + \\frac{1}{2}(70000) - 10000$\n$= 32500 + 35000 - 10000$\n$= 62500$\n\nHmm, but the direct calculation gives 57500. Let me check where the $\\alpha$ formula comes from.\n\nThe first term is:\n$\\frac{b A_Q v + (a+c) K_0 A_P u w}{A_Q v + K_0 A_P u w}$\n\n$= \\frac{\\frac{\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger}{R} k_Q + \\frac{\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}}{R} K_{eq} k_P}{k_Q + K_{eq} k_P}$\n\n$= \\frac{\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger k_Q + (\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}) K_{eq} k_P}{R(k_Q + K_{eq} k_P)}$\n\nSo $E_{app} = R \\times \\frac{\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger k_Q + (\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}) K_{eq} k_P}{R(k_Q + K_{eq} k_P)} - \\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq} K_{eq}}{1+K_{eq}}$\n\n$= \\frac{\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger k_Q + (\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}) K_{eq} k_P}{k_Q + K_{eq} k_P} - \\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq} K_{eq}}{1+K_{eq}}$\n\nOK so the first term is a weighted average of $\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger$ and $(\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq})$ with weights $k_Q$ and $K_{eq} k_P$ respectively.\n\nLet $\\alpha = \\frac{K_{eq} k_P}{k_Q + K_{eq} k_P}$, so $1-\\alpha = \\frac{k_Q}{k_Q + K_{eq} k_P}$.\n\nFirst term = $(1-\\alpha) \\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger + \\alpha (\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq})$\n\nAt $K_{eq} = 1$, $k_P = k_Q$:\n$\\alpha = 1/2$\nFirst term = $\\frac{1}{2}(70000) + \\frac{1}{2}(65000) = 35000 + 32500 = 67500$ \u2713\n\n$E_{app} = 67500 - 10000 = 57500$ J/mol = 57.5 kJ/mol\n\nSo I made an arithmetic error earlier. Let me recheck:\n$32500 + 35000 - 10000 = 57500$ \u2713\n\nYes, $E_{app}(500 \\text{ K}) = 57.5$ kJ/mol.\n\n**Part (5): Isotopically substituted reactant.**\n\nNew activation enthalpies:\n$\\Delta H_{P,iso}^\\ddagger = 45 + 4 = 49$ kJ/mol\n$\\Delta H_{Q,iso}^\\ddagger = 70 + 1 = 71$ kJ/mol\n\nActivation entropies unchanged:\n$\\Delta S_{P,iso}^\\ddagger = -20$ J/(mol\u00b7K)\n$\\Delta S_{Q,iso}^\\ddagger = 30$ J/(mol\u00b7K)\n\nPre-equilibrium unchanged:\n$\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq} = 20$ kJ/mol\n$\\Delta S^\\circ_{eq} = 40$ J/(mol\u00b7K)\n\nThe ratio $r_P/r_Q$:\n$$\\frac{r_P}{r_Q} = \\exp\\left(\\frac{\\Delta S_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta S^\\circ_{eq} - \\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger}{R}\\right) \\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq} - \\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger}{RT}\\right)$$\n\nWith the isotope substitution:\n$\\Delta S^* = -20 + 40 - 30 = -10$ J/(mol\u00b7K) (unchanged)\n$\\Delta H^*_{iso} = 49 + 20 - 71 = -2$ kJ/mol = $-2000$ J/mol\n\nSo:\n$$\\frac{r_P}{r_Q}\\bigg|_{iso} = \\exp\\left(\\frac{-10}{R}\\right) \\exp\\left(\\frac{2000}{RT}\\right)$$\n\nFor $r_P = r_Q$:\n$\\frac{-10}{R} + \\frac{2000}{RT} = 0$\n$T = \\frac{2000}{10} = 200$ K\n\nSo the new temperature is 200 K.\n\nAt $T = 500$ K:\n$$\\frac{r_P}{r_Q}\\bigg|_{iso} = \\exp\\left(\\frac{-10}{8.3144626} + \\frac{2000}{8.3144626 \\times 500}\\right)$$\n$= \\exp\\left(\\frac{-10 + 4}{8.3144626}\\right)$\n$= \\exp\\left(\\frac{-6}{8.3144626}\\right)$\n$= \\exp(-0.72164)$\n$= 0.4859$\n\nLet me verify: $2000/(8.3144626 \\times 500) = 2000/4157.2313 = 0.48109$\n$-10/8.3144626 = -1.20272$\nSum: $-1.20272 + 0.48109 = -0.72163$\n$\\exp(-0.72163) = ?$\n\n$\\exp(-0.7) = 0.49659$\n$\\exp(-0.02163) = 1 - 0.02163 + ... \\approx 0.97860$\n$\\exp(-0.72163) \\approx 0.49659 \\times 0.97860 = 0.48596$\n\nSo $r_P/r_Q \\approx 0.486$ at 500 K.\n\nThis means that at 500 K, the isotope substitution favors Q over P (since $r_P/r_Q < 1$).\n\n**Explanation:** The isotope substitution changes the activation enthalpies differentially: it increases $\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger$ by 4 kJ/mol (larger effect on the P pathway) but only $\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger$ by 1 kJ/mol (smaller effect on the Q pathway). Since the P pathway goes through the intermediate I (which involves the isotope-sensitive bond), the isotope effect is larger on that pathway. The pre-equilibrium is unchanged because the isotope substitution doesn't affect the A-I equilibrium (perhaps the bond that's isotopically substituted is not the one involved in the A-I interconversion, or the isotope effect on the equilibrium is negligible by assumption). However, since the activation barrier for P is raised more than for Q, the selectivity shifts toward Q.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The problem says \"only the activation enthalpies change\" and the pre-equilibrium remains unchanged. This is a bit unusual in real isotope effects (where equilibrium isotope effects do exist), but it's the given assumption. The key point is that the isotope substitution differentially affects the two pathways' activation barriers. The P pathway (going through I) has a larger isotope effect on its activation enthalpy (+4 kJ/mol) compared to the Q pathway (+1 kJ/mol). This means the effective barrier for P (which includes the pre-equilibrium enthalpy) increases by 4 kJ/mol, while the effective barrier for Q increases by only 1 kJ/mol. Since the P pathway had a lower effective barrier before substitution, the differential increase can reverse the selectivity.\n\nLet me now write up the complete solution.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider part (4) more carefully. The problem says \"without replacing the two parallel pathways by a single Arrhenius process.\" This means I should keep the full expression and not try to fit it to a single Arrhenius form. I think what I did is correct - I derived the exact expression for $E_{app}(T)$.\n\nLet me also double-check the general expression for $E_{app}(T)$.\n\nStarting from:\n$$k_{obs} = \\frac{k_Q + K_{eq} k_P}{1 + K_{eq}}$$\n\nwhere:\n$k_P = \\frac{k_B T}{h} e^{\\Delta S_P^\\ddagger/R} e^{-\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger/RT}$\n$k_Q = \\frac{k_B T}{h} e^{\\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger/R} e^{-\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger/RT}$\n$K_{eq} = e^{\\Delta S^\\circ_{eq}/R} e^{-\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}/RT}$\n\nNote that the $k_B T/h$ factor appears in both $k_P$ and $k_Q$ but not in $K_{eq}$. This means we can factor it out:\n\n$k_{obs} = \\frac{k_B T}{h} \\cdot \\frac{e^{\\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger/R} e^{-\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger/RT} + e^{\\Delta S^\\circ_{eq}/R} e^{\\Delta S_P^\\ddagger/R} e^{-(\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq})/RT}}{1 + e^{\\Delta S^\\circ_{eq}/R} e^{-\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}/RT}}$\n\n$= \\frac{k_B T}{h} \\cdot \\frac{e^{\\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger/R} e^{-\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger/RT} + e^{(\\Delta S^\\circ_{eq} + \\Delta S_P^\\ddagger)/R} e^{-(\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq})/RT}}{1 + e^{\\Delta S^\\circ_{eq}/R} e^{-\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}/RT}}$\n\nNow $\\ln k_{obs} = \\ln(k_B T/h) + \\ln(\\text{numerator}) - \\ln(\\text{denominator})$\n\n$\\frac{d \\ln(k_B T/h)}{d(1/T)} = \\frac{d \\ln(k_B T/h)}{dT} \\cdot \\frac{dT}{d(1/T)} = \\frac{1}{T} \\cdot (-T^2) = -T$\n\nSo the $T$-dependence of $k_B T/h$ contributes $-R \\cdot (-T) = RT$ to $E_{app}$.\n\nHmm wait, let me be more careful.\n\n$E_{app} = -R \\frac{d \\ln k_{obs}}{d(1/T)}$\n\n$\\frac{d \\ln k_{obs}}{d(1/T)} = \\frac{d}{d(1/T)} \\left[\\ln \\frac{k_B T}{h} + \\ln(\\text{num}) - \\ln(\\text{den})\\right]$\n\nFor the first term:\n$\\frac{d \\ln(k_B T/h)}{d(1/T)} = \\frac{d}{d(1/T)} \\ln T + \\text{const} = \\frac{d \\ln T}{d(1/T)}$\n\nSince $T = 1/(1/T)$, $\\ln T = -\\ln(1/T)$, so $\\frac{d \\ln T}{d(1/T)} = -1$.\n\nSo $\\frac{d \\ln(k_B T/h)}{d(1/T)} = -1$.\n\nTherefore this contributes $-R \\times (-1) = R$ to $E_{app}$.\n\nNow for the numerator and denominator, let me define:\n- $f_1 = e^{\\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger/R} e^{-\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger/RT}$ (the Q term in numerator)\n- $f_2 = e^{(\\Delta S^\\circ_{eq} + \\Delta S_P^\\ddagger)/R} e^{-(\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq})/RT}$ (the P term in numerator)\n- $g_1 = 1$\n- $g_2 = e^{\\Delta S^\\circ_{eq}/R} e^{-\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}/RT}$ (the equilibrium term in denominator)\n\nNumerator: $N = f_1 + f_2$\nDenominator: $D = g_1 + g_2 = 1 + g_2$\n\n$\\frac{d \\ln N}{d(1/T)} = \\frac{f_1' + f_2'}{f_1 + f_2}$\n\nwhere $f_1' = \\frac{d f_1}{d(1/T)} = -\\frac{\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger}{R} f_1$\nand $f_2' = \\frac{d f_2}{d(1/T)} = -\\frac{\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}}{R} f_2$\n\n$\\frac{d \\ln D}{d(1/T)} = \\frac{g_2'}{1 + g_2}$\n\nwhere $g_2' = \\frac{d g_2}{d(1/T)} = -\\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}}{R} g_2$\n\nSo:\n$\\frac{d \\ln k_{obs}}{d(1/T)} = -1 + \\frac{-\\frac{\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger}{R} f_1 - \\frac{(\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq})}{R} f_2}{f_1 + f_2} - \\frac{-\\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}}{R} g_2}{1 + g_2}$\n\n$= -1 - \\frac{\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger f_1 + (\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}) f_2}{R(f_1 + f_2)} + \\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq} g_2}{R(1 + g_2)}$\n\nTherefore:\n$E_{app} = -R \\frac{d \\ln k_{obs}}{d(1/T)} = R + \\frac{\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger f_1 + (\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}) f_2}{f_1 + f_2} - \\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq} g_2}{1 + g_2}$\n\nNow, $f_1 = e^{\\Delta S_Q^\\ddagger/R} e^{-\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger/RT}$ and $f_2 = e^{(\\Delta S^\\circ_{eq} + \\Delta S_P^\\ddagger)/R} e^{-(\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq})/RT}$.\n\nNote that $f_1$ is proportional to $k_Q$ (specifically $k_Q = \\frac{k_B T}{h} f_1$) and $f_2$ is proportional to $K_{eq} k_P$ (specifically $K_{eq} k_P = \\frac{k_B T}{h} f_2$).\n\nSo:\n$E_{app} = R + \\frac{\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger k_Q + (\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}) K_{eq} k_P}{k_Q + K_{eq} k_P} - \\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq} K_{eq}}{1 + K_{eq}}$\n\nWait, I need to check whether the $k_B T/h$ factor cancels. Since $f_1$ and $f_2$ are the \"reduced\" rate constants (without the $k_B T/h$ factor), and both have the same $k_B T/h$ prefactor in the actual rate constants, the ratio is the same. So yes, I can replace $f_1 \\propto k_Q$ and $f_2 \\propto K_{eq} k_P$ in the ratio.\n\nSo the final expression is:\n$$\\boxed{E_{app}(T) = R + \\frac{\\Delta H_Q^\\ddagger \\, k_Q(T) + (\\Delta H_P^\\ddagger + \\Delta H^\\circ_{eq}) \\, K_{eq}(T) \\, k_P(T)}{k_Q(T) + K_{eq}(T) \\, k_P(T)} - \\frac{\\Delta H^\\circ_{eq} \\, K_{eq}(T)}{1 + K_{eq}(T)}}$$\n\nLet me verify at $T = 500$ K:\n$K_{eq} = 1$, $k_P = k_Q = k$\n\n$E_{app} = R + \\frac{70000 k + 65000 k}{2k} - \\frac{20000}{2}$\n$= 8.314 + 67500 - 10000$\n$= 57508.3$ J/mol\n$\\approx 57.5$ kJ/mol\n\nThe $R$ term is negligible compared to the other terms (about 0.015%), so $E_{app} \\approx 57.5$ kJ/mol.\n\nActually, I realize I should double-check whether the $R$ term should be there or not. Let me reconsider.\n\nThe issue is that $k_{obs} = \\frac{k_B T}{h} \\cdot \\frac{N}{D}$ where $N$ and $D$ are the \"reduced\" quantities.\n\n$\\ln k_{obs} = \\ln \\frac{k_B T}{h} + \\ln N - \\ln D$\n\n$\\frac{d}{d(1/T)} \\ln \\frac{k_B T}{h} = \\frac{d}{d(1/T)} [\\ln k_B - \\ln h + \\ln T] = \\frac{d \\ln T}{d(1/T)} = -1$\n\nSo $-R \\cdot (-1) = R$ is indeed a contribution to $E_{app}$.\n\nBut wait - in the Eyring equation, the standard form already includes the $T$ factor. When we compute the \"activation energy\" from a rate constant that has a $T$-dependence beyond just the exponential, we get this extra $R$ contribution. This is actually the well-known result that for a process with $k = (k_B T/h) e^{-\\Delta G^\\ddagger/RT}$, the apparent activation energy is $E_a = \\Delta H^\\ddagger + RT$ (approximately, when $\\Delta C_P = 0$).\n\nSo the correct expression includes the $+R$ term. At 500 K, $R = 8.314$ J/mol which is negligible compared to 57500 J/mol.\n\nSo $E_{app}(500 \\text{ K}) = 57.5$ kJ/mol (to 3 significant figures).\n\nLet me now organize everything for a clean presentation.\n\nActually, I want to reconsider whether the problem is asking for a numerical value or an expression for $E_{app}(T)$ in part (4). It says \"Derive $E_{app}(T)$ from the expression obtained in part (1)\" and then \"Evaluate $E_{app}$ at 500.0 K.\" So I need both the general expression and the numerical evaluation.\n\nLet me also reconsider the $R$ term. In many textbook treatments, when people write $k = A e^{-E_a/RT}$, the $A$ is taken as temperature-independent. But in the Eyring equation, $A = (k_B T/h) e^{\\Delta S^\\ddagger/R}$, which has a $T$-dependence. So the \"activation energy\" extracted from an Arrhenius plot would be $E_a = \\Delta H^\\ddagger + RT$ (to first order in $1/T$).\n\nIn our case, since $k_{obs}$ is a more complex function, the $+R$ term is indeed present. It's a small correction.\n\nLet me present the final answer with $E_{app}(500 \\text{ K}) = 57.5$ kJ/mol (or more precisely, $57.51$ kJ/mol if we include the $R$ term).\n\nActually, let me compute it more precisely:\n$E_{app} = 8.314 + 67500 - 10000 = 57508.3$ J/mol = $57.51$ kJ/mol\n\nRounding to appropriate significant figures (given the input data has 4 significant figures), I'd say $E_{app} = 57.51$ kJ/mol or $57.5$ kJ/mol.\n\nHmm, but actually the input data gives values like 20.00, 40.00, 45.00, -20.00, 70.00, 30.00, all with 4 significant f